  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "L1/L2 records report low, poor, or <1% oral bioavailability for multiple terpenoid/steroid contexts relevant to the query: andrographolide poor in humans and 0.3% (C13, C18), docetaxel low absolute F in humans and 0.315% in rats (C08, C23), withaferin A poor in humans and ~9% in mice (C10, C25), fluticasone <1% (C07, C15, C28), mometasone <1%/negligible (C02, C04, C24), anecortave low/very low (C05, C27, C29), Rg3 low/2.63% (C14, C26), rifaximin <0.4% (C01, C17), and sitostanol 0.04% (C03). These support F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C03","C04","C05","C07","C08","C10","C13","C14","C15","C17","C18","C23","C24","C25","C26","C27","C28","C29"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records show very low plasma exposure after oral dosing in humans for structurally related terpenoid acids: abiraterone acetate Cmax ~54.67 pg/mL and AUCt/AUCinf ~460/386 pg·h/mL (C36, C39, C34, C41, C37); pachymic acid Cmax 36.3–91.0 pg/mL after 2.5–7.5 g rikkunshito (C32, C33, C30, C31, C35, C38, C40). These are consistent with poor oral absorption/high first-pass, though they lack IV comparators and are not direct F values.",
      "card_ids": ["C30","C31","C32","C33","C34","C35","C36","C37","C38","C39","C40","C41"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic stability records show high CYP-mediated intrinsic clearance and short metabolic half-lives for related tetracyclic terpenoids/steroids: 20(S)-protopanaxadiol t1/2 6 min in human liver microsomes with CYP3A4/3A5 oxidation (C66, C72), cinobufagin intrinsic clearance 47.2–62.0 µL/min/mg (C74, C77), and testosterone/epitestosterone predicted hepatic clearance 9.7–14.11 mL/min/kg (C75, C68). This mechanistically supports hepatic first-pass loss, but is not query-specific and is not direct oral-bioavailability evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C66","C68","C72","C74","C75","C77"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No affirmative evidence supports F >= 20% for the query; the single high withaferin A rat F (87%, C16) is species- and formulation-specific and conflicts with the human poor outcome (C10).",
      "card_ids": ["C16","C10"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C66","C68","C69","C70","C71","C72","C73","C74","C75","C76","C77"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic stability assay is available; L6 records are for other terpenoids/steroids and are mechanistic context only.",
    "L3 exposure records lack IV comparators and dose-normalized AUC, so very low pg/mL Cmax/AUC is indirect rather than direct F evidence.",
    "Structural similarity to low-bioavailability contexts is qualitative; exact scaffold and functional-group match is not established.",
    "Species and formulation differences, such as withaferin A 87% in rats versus poor in humans, create uncertainty.",
    "Physicochemical prior is mixed; fully ionized carboxylic acid and high MW suggest absorption barriers, but logD 2.56 and TPSA 104 are not definitive."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains low; L6 hepatic clearance/metabolic stability records add mechanistic support for hepatic first-pass loss in related terpenoid/steroid scaffolds, but are not query-specific. Combined with L1/L2 direct low/poor F outcomes and L3 very low oral exposure, F < 20% is better supported. No affirmative evidence supports F >= 20%."
}

